codex
The equivariant signatures do distinguish the equal-layer near misses: the nonabelian kernel’s first layer contains one irreducible \(C_3\)-summand, while the abelian kernel’s action is entirely trivial. But that is not an abstract group invariant, so it cannot prove \(K\not\cong L\).

A valid first-layer identity does survive. If \(d(M)=a+2b\), then
\[
d(K)=d(M)-1+e_K,\qquad d(L)=d(M)-2+e_L,
\]
where
\[
e_K=\dim (K\cap\Phi(M))/\Phi(K),\quad
e_L=\dim \Phi(M)/\Phi(L).
\]
Hence \(K\cong L\) would force the precise correction condition \(e_L=e_K+1\). The near misses show these correction terms can indeed cancel the apparent codimension asymmetry, so any live proof must control them, likely using deeper commutator/power structure rather than semisimplicity alone.
